(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "2607314000")
  (:adr/title "The -rdfc- cryptosuite plane, closed")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-07-31")
  (:adr/supersedes "2607309400 (RDFC portion only)")
  (:adr/body "# ADR-2607314000 \u2014 the -rdfc- cryptosuite plane, closed\n\n## Status\n\nThe `eddsa-rdfc-2022` chain is complete, externally validated, and reachable from the\napplication. This ADR supersedes the \"tower under construction\" framing in\nADR-2607309400 for the RDFC half; that ADR remains authoritative for the app's\noverall credential design.\n\n## What exists on default branches\n\nSix repositories, every commit below merged to `main` and verified server-side as\nreachable with `behind=0`:\n\n| repo | pin | what it is |\n|---|---|---|\n| `io-multiformats` | `dc52003` | SHA-384 (RDFC-1.0's optional hash) |\n| `org-w3-nquads` | `794846c` | canonical N-Triples serialization |\n| `org-w3-rdf-canon` | `a869606` | RDFC-1.0, complete incl. N-degree |\n| `org-w3-json-ld-api` | `4a8d2f3` | JSON-LD 1.1 expansion + \u00a77 RDF conversion |\n| `org-w3-vc-data-integrity` | `37ccc89` | `eddsa-rdfc-2022` + suite dispatch |\n| `cloud-itonami-app` | `cce3cb4` | verifies both cryptosuites |\n\n## The verification that matters\n\n`eddsa-rdfc-2022` is pinned to W3C's own vector (vc-di-eddsa Appendix B.1), and\nEVERY intermediate value reproduces byte for byte \u2014 not just the final proofValue,\nbecause one matching number can hide two errors that cancelled:\n\n    canonical document      517744132ae165a5349155bef0bb0cf2258fff99dfe1dbd914b938d775a36017\n    canonical proof config  bea7b7acfbad0126b135104024a5f1733e705108f42d59668b05c0c50004c6b0\n    hashData                bea7b7ac\u2026 \u2016 51774413\u2026   (proof config FIRST)\n    proofValue              z2YwC8z3ap7yx1nZYCg4L3j3ApHsF8kgPdSb5xoS1VR7vPG3F561B52hYnQF9ise\u2026\n\nBelow that: RDFC-1.0 passes 64/64 eval and 21/21 map vectors and REFUSES the poison\nclique as its negative test requires. JSON-LD expansion is at 123/273 positive and\n51/103 negative; \u00a77 conversion at 154/340. Those last figures are partial and stated\nas such \u2014 see Gaps.\n\n## Decisions worth carrying forward\n\n### Contexts are never fetched\n\n`org-w3-json-ld-api` does no network I/O and cannot be configured to. A fetched\n`@context` lets its host change what a signature covers AFTER signing, with no key\nand nothing to detect \u2014 the document still verifies, against a different graph. The\napp pins the published credentials/v2 bytes\n(sha256 `59955ced6697d61e03f2b2556febe5308ab16842846f5b586d7f1f7adec92734`).\n\nOperational consequence, stated where the bytes live: an issuer and a verifier who\npin DIFFERENT bytes for the same URL disagree about the graph and the proof fails\nwith no indication why.\n\n### Dispatch is an allowlist, never the proof's own claim\n\n`cryptosuite` is a field of the proof, chosen by whoever produced the document.\nSelecting a suite from it unconditionally is a downgrade attack needing no forgery.\n`:accept-suites` is therefore an explicit allowlist; the empty allowlist accepts\nnothing. Without dispatch a mixed corpus was unverifiable, which is what blocked any\nmigration off `eddsa-jcs-2022` \u2014 not the cryptosuite itself.\n\n### Refuse rather than guess, and refuse at USE\n\nUnimplemented constructs throw with the spec's own error code. An expansion missing\ntriples would be SIGNED, so silence is worse than failure. But refusals belong where\nthey change the output: refusing at context-processing time made credentials/v2\nunusable over `@json` on three terms no credential touches.\n\n### The app's own terms needed real IRIs\n\n`credentials/v2` defines none of `role`/`organizationName`/`organization` and sets no\n`@vocab`, so under any `-rdfc-` suite expansion DROPPED them: the canonical RDF of a\nmembership credential contained no role. Invisible under JCS, which signs the JSON.\nFixed with an inline `@context` over `urn:cloud-itonami:credentials:membership:v1#`.\nA correctness fix for today, not preparation.\n\n## Gaps, honestly\n\n### Blocked on owner decisions\n\n1. **did:web form** \u2014 path-based `did:web:hooks.itonami.cloud:orgs:<id>` (works\n   today) versus `<org>.gftd.ai` (needs DNS). Every credential names it; changing it\n   later invalidates them all. Raised in 35 consecutive iterations, never answered.\n2. **Holder key** \u2014 a software Ed25519 key, or WebAuthn treated as its own binding\n   mechanism. Blocks Verifiable Presentations. The structural reason is unchanged: a\n   Passkey signs its own `authenticatorData \u2016 clientDataHash`, so it cannot produce a\n   Data Integrity proof, and no cryptosuite changes that. `ecdsa-jcs-2019` IS\n   implemented \u2014 an earlier docstring wrongly said otherwise.\n3. **Issuance cutover** \u2014 the app verifies both suites but still issues JCS. rdfc\n   issuance can FAIL where JCS cannot (missing context, work limit), so turning it on\n   is a deployment decision, now a one-line change.\n\n### Mine, measured\n\n- JSON-LD expansion: 42 positive mismatches, 25 malformed documents still accepted.\n  `expand` is NOT a validator and says so in three places. Remaining clusters:\n  `tc012`/`tc019` (property-scoped context nested in a type-scoped one) and six\n  `tli*` coerced-@list cases.\n- RDFC-1.0 N-degree is complete, but `:max-work` bounds it by spec MUST; the margin\n  is measured (heaviest legitimate vector 3,348 units, default 100,000).\n- 7 files under `90-docs/` still do not parse. They need structural reconstruction\n  (missing opening brackets), not delimiter repair \u2014 diagnosis is in the commit for\n  `fix/edn-parse-repair-2`.\n\n## What this exercise kept teaching\n\nThe same defect recurred four times: a layer correct in isolation and unwired above\nit. The app's revocation check, the app's credential context, `eddsa-rdfc-2022`\nunreachable through `issue`, and a mixed corpus unverifiable. A component test proves\nan algorithm; only a call through the public entry point proves anyone can use it.\n\nTwice a rising suite total masked an unfixed case, and once my own harness was\ncounting ten non-bugs against the library. Counting the total is not checking the\ncase.")))
